          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : Conf \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            that is invariant under a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of symmetries
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} acting
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Conf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall g \in G, \phi \in Conf : \,\,\, S(g(\phi)) = S(\phi)
          %p
            a solution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
            of motion is said to exhibit
            %strong spontaneously broken symmetry
            if it is not a fixed-point of that group
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≠
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(\phi_0) \neq \phi_0
          %p
            The “breaking” refers to the fact that the group no longer acts. It is called “spontaneous” because one imagines that by a physical process the theory “finds” one of its solutions. This comes from the class of examples where a statistical system is first considered at high temperature and then cooled down. At some point it will “spontaneously” freeze in one allowed configuration. A standard example is a
            = succeed ":" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                ferromagnet
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/ferromagnet"} ?
            %span.newWikiWord
              magnetization
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/magnetization"} ?
            vanishes, while at very low temperature it spontaneously finds a direction of magnetization, thus “breaking” rotational symmetry.
          %p
            One calls the subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 0
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\phi_0} \subset G
            that fixes the given configuration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            the
            %em unbroken symmetry group
            \.
          %p
            In the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            arising by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of this action functional one considers the given classical solution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            as a background about which to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbations
            of the remaining
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
          %p
            The fields in this effective QFT are then small excitations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
            around the given
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mi δ
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0 + \delta \phi
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\phi_0}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            send
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi g
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g : (\phi_0 + \delta \phi) \mapsto g(\phi_0) + g(\delta \phi)
                  \= \phi_0 + g \delta \phi
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since in the effective theory around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            state where all the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \phi
            have no excitations (or rather: are in their ground state) corresponds to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            itself one says in this context that
            %em a quantum theory exhibits spontaneouly broken symmetry if its vacuum state is not invariant under the pertinent symmetries
            \.
          %h2#FormalizaitonInCohesion Formalization in cohesive homotopy-type theory
          %p
            We indicate the formalization of the concept in the axiomatics of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            be a cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+infinity-group"} abelian infinity-group
            (for instance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth cohesion
          %p
            Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulating+morphism"} modulating morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔾
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"} principal connections
          %p
            A symmetry of the theory means that there is a cohesive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-group"} infinity-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"} infinity-acting
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            such that the prequantum bundle descends to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔾
                            %mi conn
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P  &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  P/G
                  }
          %p
            Now given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"} infinity-action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G}
            on some
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔾
                  %mo =
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G} = U(1)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \mathbb{C}
            for traditional quantum mechanics) then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+infinity-bundle"} associated infinity-bundle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi Ψ
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi 𝔾
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝔾
                            %mi conn
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi 𝔾
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P &amp;\stackrel{\Psi}{\longrightarrow}&amp; V/\mathbb{G}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;\swArrow_{\simeq}&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{conn}
                  \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                  \\mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}
                  }
          %p
            So this is something defined on phase space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P/G
            (this is hard to draw here, but should be clear) then that makes the wavefunction also
            = succeed "-equivariant." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            “breaks” the
            = succeed "-symmetry." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Now if on top of this we have that the given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            is a “ground state”, then if it does not descend to the homotopy quotient we say “the
            = succeed "-symmetry" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            To axiomatize what “ground state” means: introduce another
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            which is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            to (be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            and) be a minimal eigenstate of the respective Hamiltonian. That makes it a “ground state”.
          %p
            For more on the general translation between traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive
            homotopy theory see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Higher+geometric+prequantum+theory"} Higher geometric prequantum theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#scalars_in_mexican_hat_potential Scalars in mexican hat potential
          %p
            A standard example which is both very simple but at the same time of central importance in one of the main applications in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+symmetry+breaking"} electroweak symmetry breaking
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"} Higgs mechanism
            – is this:
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Conf
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf = C^\infty(X = [0,1]^d, \mathbb{R}^N)
            be the configuration space of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            real scalar fields and take the action functional to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi ϕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mi h
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ϕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mi g
                      %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ϕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 4
                    %mo )
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \phi \mapsto \int_X \left( -\frac{1}{2}\vert \nabla \phi \vert^2
                  \-
                  \\frac{h}{2} \vert\phi\vert^2 - \frac{g}{4} \vert\phi\vert^4
                  \\right) d\mu_X
          %p
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h, g \in \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = O(N)
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Conf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Conf
          %p
            This action functional has a class of critical points given by constant maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Φ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \to \mathbb{R}^n : \phi(x) = \Phi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            precisely if the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            extremize the potential energy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi h
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ϕ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mfrac
                    %mi g
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi ϕ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{h}{2} \vert\phi\vert^2 + \frac{g}{4} \vert\phi\vert^4
                  \\,.
          %p
            If both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            are positive, then there is only one such critical point, given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi = 0
            %em not
            break the symmetry, in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Φ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g( \Phi = 0) = (\Phi = 0)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in O(N)
          %p
            However, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is negative and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            positive, then the solutions are all those
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
            with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi Φ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mfrac
                    %mi h
                    %mi g
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vert \Phi \vert^2 = - \frac{h}{g} \gt 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            The set of all these is closed under the action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = O(N)
            – this group takes one of these solutions into another – but none of these solutions is
            %em fixed
            by this action.
          %p
            One says in this case that any such solution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : x \mapsto \Phi
            is a solution that
            %em spontaneously breaks the symmetry
            of the theory.
          %h3#in_gravity In gravity
          %p
            The theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            on a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configurations
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            or one of its variants – is invariant under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            group on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The corresponding
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            %em breaks
            the symmetry given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to X
            unless
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector+field"} Killing vector field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
          %h4#kaluzaklein_reductions Kaluza-Klein reductions
          %p
            Spontaneous symmetry breaking in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            plays a central role for instance in the context of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim X = 5
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            of gravity around a solution of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X = X_4 \times S^1, g_4 \otimes g_1)
            is 4-dimensional gravity coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            (and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilaton"} dilaton
            field): the components of the field of gravity along the circle transmute into the electromagnetic field. The ansatz
            %em breaks
            all the symmetries that would mix the remaining 4-dimensional gravitational excitations with these new electromagnetic excitations.
          %p
            This is discussed in a bit of detail for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Strominger"}> Strominger, lecture 1
            ).
          %h4#super_kaluzaklein_reductions Super Kaluza-Klein reductions
          %p
            The above discussion has a direct analog in theories of higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            around classical solutions that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein reductions
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^4 \times Y^d, (g_4 \otimes g_d))
            exhibits as global symmetries all those
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            %em spontaneously broken
            by this solution.
          %p
            In this case, though, there are also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            analogs of the plain diffeomorphism action. Such a local supersymmetry remains unbroken in the given solution if it comes from a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinor+field"} Killing spinor field
          %p
            Therefore KK-reductions to 4-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
            in supergravity that admit precisely four Killing spinors of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 4
                  %mo =
                  %mi const
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mi covariantly
                  %mi const
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi_4 = const \otimes \psi_d = covariantly const)
            give rise to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theories"} effective field theories
            with exactly one remaining global
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+breaking"} supersymmetry breaking
          %p
            For more see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau manifolds
          %p
            This is discussed in a bit of detail for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Strominger"}> Strominger, lecture 2
            ).
          %h4#scherkschwarz_mechanism Scherk-Schwarz mechanism
          %p
            Specifically, the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scherk-Schwarz+mechanism"} Scherk-Schwarz mechanism
            (
            %a{:href => "#ScherkSchwarz79"}> Scherk-Schwarz 79
            ) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneously+broken+symmetry"} spontaneous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+breaking"} supersymmetry breaking
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
            imposes anti-periodic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+group"}> global gauge group
                  ,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+gauge+group"} local gauge group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goldstone+boson"} Goldstone boson
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} topological defects
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+wall"}> domain wall
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+string"} cosmic string
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopole"} monopole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+gauge+symmetry"} enhanced gauge symmetry
          %h2#references References
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
              ,
              %em The quantum theory of fields
          %p
            sponaneously broken
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+group"} global gauge group
            symmetry is discussed in vol I, section 19, and spontaneously broken
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+gauge+group"} local gauge group
            symmetry in vol I, section 21.4.
          %p Survey:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Jose Bernabeu,
                %em Symmetries and their breaking in the fundamental laws of physics
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.13996"}> arXiv:2006.13996
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom%C3%A1%C5%A1+Brauner"}> Tomáš Brauner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Effective Field Theory for Spontaneously Broken Symmetry
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 1023
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14518"} arXiv:2404.14518
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-48378-3"} doi:10.1007/978-3-031-48378-3
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (perspective of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
          %p
            Textbook discussion of broken symmetry in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein mechanism
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Strominger
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Strominger"} Andrew Strominger
              (notes by
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Morgan"} John Morgan
              %em Kaluza-Klein compactifications, Supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau spaces
              , volume II, starting on page 1091 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Fields+and+Strings"}> Quantum Fields and Strings
                  , A course for mathematicians
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/qft"} web version
          %p
            Discussion of spontaneous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+breaking"} supersymmetry breaking
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Yael Shadmi,
                %em Supersymmetry breaking
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0601076"}> arXiv:hep-th/0601076
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Polchinski"}> Joseph Polchinski
                , volume II, appendix B of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+theory"} String theory
          %p The article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaas+Landsman"}> Klaas Landsman
              , Robin Reuvers,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Flea on Schrödinger’s Cat
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2353"} arXiv:1210.2353
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ru.nl/~landsman/Catresubmission.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            points out that for symmetric systems with a symmetric ground state, already a tiny perturbation mixing the ground state with the first excited stated causes spontaneous symmetry breaking in the suitable limit, and suggests that this already resolves the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement+problem"} measurement problem
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
